    hero: {
      title: 'SPIZZICHINO',
      subtitle: 'Card Game',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Spizzichino',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Spizzichino (or 'Pizzichino') is a complex two-player Italian card game played with a 40-card deck (Italian suits). It combines elements of Briscola (trick-taking) and Scopa (capture/fishing) with meld-making (similar to Bezique/Pinochle). The name likely refers to 'picking' or 'nibbling' at points in small increments. The game is highly strategic and lesser-known outside Italy.",
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dates to the 18th or 19th century. A hybrid of Tressette mechanics (ranking 3-2-A) and Scopa capture? No, usually Tressette ranking.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Two players; they take turns to deal. Each player has a hand of 10 cards and two face-up packets of 5 cards on the table (only the top card of each packet is visible). Play is from hand or from the top of a packet.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40 cards (Italian or Stripped 52).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40-card Italian pack (Latin suits). In some regions a 40-card French pack with Queen replacing Cavallo.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer shuffles and creates eight packets of 5 cards face down. Opponent chooses four packets: two placed face-up on the table (top card visible only), two picked up as a 10-card hand. Dealer does the same with the other four. Each player has a 10-card hand and two face-up packets of 5 cards.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Look at bottom of packets: Before choosing which packets to turn up, each player may look at the bottom card of their own packets.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'No buongiochi: Buongiochi are not declared or scored; game is played to 21 or 31 points instead of 51.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Tressette in Due a Metà Mazzo: Alternative variant: deal 10 each, 20 cards as stock. After each trick both players draw from stock (winner first) and show the card. Declare buongiochi before first trick or when a draw completes a new combination. Game to 21, 31, 41 or 51.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
